      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Reinhardt's Theorem 5 states the inconsistency of a schema over arbitrary formulas p(x) and a two-variable formula U(e,x), whereas Target specializes to the single diagonal instance p(x) := ¬U(x,x) used in the proof (a harmless strengthening for proving the schema inconsistent) and fixes U to a single primitive binary relation symbol (atom 0) rather than an arbitrary two-variable formula. Both limitations are noted in the author's claim."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Compared to the full system in [P-R323], the context uses a pure first-order modal fragment without arithmetic, equality, constants, or function symbols (a declared limitation, and a harmless strengthening for proving inconsistency), and Def13 permits K ψ for open formulas ψ ∈ Def12 rather than only closed sentences (equivalent in the presence of kall and inst)."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Target is divergent from Reinhardt's Theorem 5 because it omits the outer modal operator B (K) in ∃e B∀x(B¬U(x,x) ↔ U(e,x)), instead asserting the refutability of ∃e ∀x(B¬U(x,x) ↔ U(e,x)). That omission fundamentally alters the claim and renders Target unprovable (false) in the defined logic, since the formula without the outer K is consistent."
